This demo aims at showcasing:[
  1. How the technologies used in it work. 
  2. My epistemological approach to SaaS documentation.
  3. The advantages of using a live Knowledge Base as opposed to a PDF file.
 
]

## Technologies used in this demo

The content itself is written in Markdown (a markup language like HTML but simpler, lighter, and more readable, it is the industry standard for documentation).
So this is how it looks like as I am writting this content using Visual Studio Code as the editor

(screenshot here)

once the content is written I run Docusaurus (a static site generator) to convert the Markdown files into a website.

Docusausus is a free, open-source engine contributed by Meta to the open source community. 
I download and install docusaurus on my computer.
Docusaurus is React based, so it uses javascript and React to render the content into a lightweight, fast, and responsive website.

I have git and github (version control) on my computer so I have added the project folder to a git repository and pushed it to my github account.

Then I used Vercel to deploy the website. Vercel is a cloud platform for static sites and serverless functions that fits perfectly with Docusaurus. 
It is free for personal projects and it is very easy to use. However, the website can also be deployed on GitHub Pages, which is a free hosting service 
for static websites provided by GitHub. It can also be deployed on any other paid hosting services that supports static websites (for example an azure web app).

### Production Flow

I create documentation content in markdown, save the changes (Visual Studio Code). I preview the changes in my local environment (Docusaurus). 
If the changes are satisfactory, I commit the changes to git (git) and push the changes to github (github).
Vercel automatically detects the changes in the repo and rebuild the website.

